  "page_text": "                                                                                    \n                                                                                    \n                                                                                    \n     INSTALLATION:   CARBON                PRESSURE    RELIEF VALVE                 \n     GRAPHITE   BUSHINGS                   INSTRUCTIONS                             \n                                                                                    \n     When installing carbon graphite bushings, extreme care                         \n     must be taken to prevent breaking. Carbon graphite is a                        \n                                                FIGURE 12: RELIEF VALVE - ALL SIZES \n     brittle material and easily cracked. If cracked, the bushing will              \n                                                  NOTE: Image is representative only.\n     quickly disintegrate. Using a lubricant and adding a chamfer                   \n     on the bushing and the mating part will help in installation.                  \n                                                          V8  V6 V5 V4 V3 V1        \n     The additional precautions listed below must be followed for                   \n     proper installation.                                   V7          V2          \n     1. A press must be used for installation.                       V9             \n     2. Be certain the bushing is started straight.                                 \n     3. Do not stop pressing operation until bushing is in proper                   \n       position. Starting and stopping will result in a cracked                     \n       bushing.                                                                     \n     4. Check bushing for cracks after installation.                                \n     Carbon graphite bushings with extra interference fits are                      \n                                                                        A           \n     frequently furnished for high temperature operation. These                     \n     bushings must be installed by a shrink fit.                     V10            \n     1. Heat bracket or idler to 750°F.                                             \n                                                       Valve - List Of Parts        \n     2. Install cool bushing with a press.                                          \n                                             V1. Valve Cap   V6. Valve Body         \n     3. If facilities are not available to reach 750°F. temperature,                \n                                             V2. Adjusting Screw V7. Valve Spring(s)\n       it is possible to install with 450°F. temperature; however                   \n                                             V3. Lock Nut    V8. Poppet             \n       the lower the temperature the greater the possibility of                     \n       cracking the bushing.                 V4. Spring Guide V9. Cap Gasket        \n     Consult your Viking Pump® representative with specific V5. Bonnet V10. Bonnet Gasket*\n     questions on high temperature applications.                                    \n                                                         * K, KK, L, LQ, LL, LS, Q, QS sizes only\n     NOTE: Bronze and hardened cast iron bushings can be                            \n     pressed into the mating part. Use steps 1 and 2 above.                         \n                                                     ⚠                              \n                                                         DANGER   !                 \n                                           Before opening any Viking pump liquid chamber\n                                           (pumping chamber, reservoir, relief valve adjusting cap\n                                           fitting, etc.) be sure:                  \n                                           1. That any pressure in the chamber has been\n                                             completely vented through the suction or discharge\n                                             lines, or other appropriate openings or connections.\n                                           2. That the driving means (motor, turbine, engine, etc.)\n                                             has been “locked out” or made non-operational, so that\n                                             it cannot be started while work is being done on pump.\n                                           3. That you know what liquid the pump has been handling\n                                             and the precautions necessary to safely handle the\n                                             liquid. Obtain a material safety data sheet (MSDS) for\n                                             the liquid to be sure these precautions are understood.\n                                           Failure to follow above listed precautionary measures\n                                           may result in serious injury or death.   \n                                           DISASSEMBLY                              \n                                           Mark valve and head before disassembly to ensure proper\n                                           reassembly.                              \n                                           1. Remove valve cap.                     \n                                           2. Measure and record length of extension of adjusting\n                                             screw. Refer to “A” on \"Figure 12\" on page 10.\n                                           3. Loosen locknut and back out adjusting screw until spring\n                                             pressure is released.                  \n                                           4. Remove bonnet, spring guide, spring and poppet from\n                                             valve body. Clean and inspect all parts for wear or damage\n                                             and replace if necessary.              \n              NOTE: 226A SERIES™ & 4226A SERIES™ JACKETED PUMPS DISCONTINUED AS OF 4Q20.\n     TSM 1600 | Issue C | Page 10 of 20               © 2020 Viking Pump, Inc. • Cedar Falls, IA",
  "page_text_sha256": "6f054403993b476fa8c5412eec969972bccc5c2c81137903ee463c50fa191940",
